a. What is duck typing, as explained in the video?
Duck typing is a concept in Python based on the saying, "If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck". It means Python does not check what class an object comes from or require inheritance. As long as an object has the needed method, Python can use it.

b. What did the Car class need to have so it could work inside the same loop as Dog and Cat, even if it does not inherit from the Animal class?
The Car class simply needed to have the exact same method name such as speak() or make_sound() as the Dog and Cat classes.

3. How does the code in 2.2 demonstrate polymorphism?
The code demonstrates polymorphism because Car, Boat, and Drone are three completely separate classes with no parent-child inheritance, yet they all share the same move() method name. When the start_trip(vehicle) function calls vehicle.move(), Python automatically runs the specific move() behavior belonging to whichever vehicle object was passed into it.
